DENGUE MODEL

Overview
The proposed framework begins with data preprocessing, where missing values were handled to ensure the quality of the data.Feature engineering was then performed
by grouping data on a weekly basis, and instead of keeping raw numbers, the total case counts turn into categorical risk levels (high, medium, low). A seasonal
variable was derived from monthly data, and additional features such as hygiene ratio, rainfall lag (Lag-1), and a relational term between rainfall and humidity
were generated to capture environmental effects. Categorical variables were encoded using one-hot and label encoding techniques. To address class imbalance, the
Synthetic Minority Oversampling Technique (SMOTE) was applied. Finally, the dataset was split into training (70%) and testing (30%) sets, and logistic regression,
decision tree, XGBoost, and random forest models were implemented for outbreak prediction.

